## Object Pooling (Java)

**Object Pooling** is a design pattern where **a pool of reusable objects is maintained so that objects can be reused instead of creating and destroying them repeatedly**.

It improves **performance and resource management**.

---

## Basic Idea

```text id="2gn0g3"
Object Pool
   ↓
Client borrows object
   ↓
Uses object
   ↓
Returns object to pool
```

Objects are **reused instead of recreated**.

---

## Example Problem Without Object Pooling

```java id="qbnnhj"
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args){

        for(int i=0;i<100000;i++){

            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

            sb.append("Java");

        }

    }

}
```

Problem:

```text id="uj7qz5"
Frequent object creation
High GC overhead
Performance issues
```

---

## Simple Object Pool Example

```java id="hz1v0j"
import java.util.*;

class ObjectPool {

    private Queue<StringBuilder> pool = new LinkedList<>();

    public ObjectPool(int size){

        for(int i=0;i<size;i++){
            pool.add(new StringBuilder());
        }

    }

    public StringBuilder borrowObject(){

        return pool.poll();

    }

    public void returnObject(StringBuilder obj){

        obj.setLength(0);
        pool.offer(obj);

    }

}
```

Usage:

```java id="t3ir92"
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args){

        ObjectPool pool = new ObjectPool(5);

        StringBuilder sb = pool.borrowObject();

        sb.append("Hello");

        System.out.println(sb);

        pool.returnObject(sb);

    }

}
```

---

## Real Examples of Object Pooling

Common real-world object pools:

```text id="n3s3rk"
Database connection pool
Thread pools
Socket pools
Buffer pools
```

Example:

```java id="hgyuf5"
ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
```

Thread pool is an **object pool of threads**.

---

## Database Connection Pool Example

Used in frameworks like:

```text id="1g0qgy"
HikariCP
Apache DBCP
C3P0
```

Example:

```java id="k22mha"
Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
```

Connection is taken from pool.

---

## Advantages

```text id="p2p24h"
Improves performance
Reduces object creation overhead
Reduces GC pressure
Efficient resource management
```

---

## Disadvantages

```text id="78vdve"
Complex implementation
Pool management overhead
Memory consumption
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Object Pooling is a design pattern where a pool of reusable objects is maintained so that objects can be borrowed and returned instead of creating new instances repeatedly.**

## Default Methods in Interfaces (Java)

**Default methods** are methods in an interface that **have a method body (implementation)**.

Introduced in **Java 8** to allow **interfaces to evolve without breaking existing implementations**.

Syntax uses the `default` keyword.

---

## Basic Example

```java
interface Vehicle {

    void start();

    default void stop() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle stopped");
    }

}
```

Implementation class:

```java
class Car implements Vehicle {

    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Car car = new Car();

        car.start();
        car.stop();

    }
}
```

Output

```
Car started
Vehicle stopped
```

---

## Why Default Methods Were Introduced

Before Java 8, adding a method to an interface would **break all implementing classes**.

Example:

Old interface:

```java
interface Payment {

    void pay();

}
```

If we add a new method:

```java
void refund();
```

All classes implementing `Payment` would break.

Solution using default method:

```java
interface Payment {

    void pay();

    default void refund() {
        System.out.println("Default refund");
    }

}
```

Now existing classes continue working.

---

## Overriding Default Method

Implementing class can override it.

```java
class Car implements Vehicle {

    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }

    public void stop() {
        System.out.println("Car stopped");
    }

}
```

---

## Default Method Conflict (Diamond Problem)

If two interfaces have the same default method:

```java
interface A {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }

}

interface B {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }

}
```

Child class must resolve:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        A.super.show();
    }

}
```

---

## Key Characteristics

```
Defined in interfaces
Have implementation
Inherited by implementing classes
Can be overridden
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Default methods are methods in interfaces that include a method body using the `default` keyword, allowing interfaces to add new functionality without breaking existing implementations.**

## SOLID Principles (Java)

**SOLID = 5 design principles used to build maintainable, scalable, and flexible object-oriented systems.**

```
S → Single Responsibility Principle
O → Open Closed Principle
L → Liskov Substitution Principle
I → Interface Segregation Principle
D → Dependency Inversion Principle
```

---

# 1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

**A class should have only one reason to change.**

Bad design:

```java
class Report {

    void generateReport() {
        System.out.println("Generating report");
    }

    void saveToFile() {
        System.out.println("Saving report");
    }

}
```

Better design:

```java
class ReportGenerator {

    void generate() {
        System.out.println("Generating report");
    }

}

class ReportSaver {

    void save() {
        System.out.println("Saving report");
    }

}
```

---

# 2. Open Closed Principle (OCP)

**Software should be open for extension but closed for modification.**

Example:

```java
interface Payment {

    void pay(double amount);

}
```

Implementations:

```java
class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Credit Card Payment: " + amount);
    }

}

class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("UPI Payment: " + amount);
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Payment payment = new UpiPayment();
payment.pay(500);
```

New payment method → **add new class without modifying existing code**.

---

# 3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

**Child classes must be replaceable with parent classes without breaking behavior.**

Example:

```java
class Bird {

    void fly() { }

}
```

Bad design:

```java
class Ostrich extends Bird {

    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }

}
```

Better design:

```java
interface Bird { }

interface FlyingBird extends Bird {
    void fly();
}
```

---

# 4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

**Clients should not be forced to implement methods they do not use.**

Bad design:

```java
interface Worker {

    void work();
    void eat();

}
```

Better design:

```java
interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}
```

---

# 5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

**High-level modules should depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations.**

Bad design:

```java
class MySQLDatabase {

    void connect() { }

}

class UserService {

    private MySQLDatabase db = new MySQLDatabase();

}
```

Better design:

```java
interface Database {
    void connect();
}
```

Implementation:

```java
class MySQLDatabase implements Database {

    public void connect() {
        System.out.println("MySQL connected");
    }

}
```

Service:

```java
class UserService {

    private Database db;

    UserService(Database db) {
        this.db = db;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Database db = new MySQLDatabase();
UserService service = new UserService(db);
```

---

## Quick Summary

| Principle | Meaning |
|---|---|
| SRP | One responsibility per class |
| OCP | Extend behavior without modifying code |
| LSP | Child objects must replace parent safely |
| ISP | Smaller, specific interfaces |
| DIP | Depend on abstractions |

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**SOLID principles are five object-oriented design principles (SRP, OCP, LSP, ISP, DIP) that help developers build maintainable, flexible, and scalable software systems.**

