In Java, `==` and `equals()` are both used for **comparison**, but they compare **different things**.

---

## 1. `==` Operator

`==` compares **references (memory addresses)** for objects and **values for primitives**.

Example (Primitive):

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int a = 10;
        int b = 10;

        System.out.println(a == b);  // true

    }

}
```

Example (Objects):

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String s1 = new String("java");
        String s2 = new String("java");

        System.out.println(s1 == s2); // false

    }

}
```

Reason:

```
s1 ----> object1
s2 ----> object2
```

Different memory locations.

---

## 2. `equals()` Method

`equals()` compares **content / logical equality**.

Example:

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String s1 = new String("java");
        String s2 = new String("java");

        System.out.println(s1.equals(s2)); // true

    }

}
```

Because:

```
content of s1 = "java"
content of s2 = "java"
```

---

## 3. String Pool Example

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String a = "hello";
        String b = "hello";

        System.out.println(a == b);        // true
        System.out.println(a.equals(b));   // true

    }

}
```

Reason:

```
String pool

"hello"
  ↑   ↑
  a   b
```

Both references point to **same pooled object**.

---

## 4. Custom Class Example

```java
class User {

    int id;

    User(int id){
        this.id = id;
    }

}

public class Test {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        User u1 = new User(1);
        User u2 = new User(1);

        System.out.println(u1 == u2); // false
        System.out.println(u1.equals(u2)); // false

    }

}
```

To compare content:

```java
@Override
public boolean equals(Object obj) {

    if(this == obj) return true;

    if(obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;

    User user = (User) obj;

    return id == user.id;
}
```

---

## Key Differences

| Feature | `==` | `equals()` |
|------|------|------|
| Type | Operator | Method |
| Compares | Memory address | Object content |
| Works on primitives | Yes | No |
| Overridable | No | Yes |

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**`==` compares memory references (or primitive values), while `equals()` compares the logical content of objects.**

## Method Reference (Java)

A **method reference** is a **shorthand way of referring to a method using `::` operator** instead of writing a lambda expression.

It is used when a **lambda only calls an existing method**.

Syntax:

```java
ClassName::methodName
```

---

# Example with Lambda

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        List<String> list = List.of("Java","Spring","React");

        list.forEach(s -> System.out.println(s));

    }
}
```

---

# Same Example with Method Reference

```java
list.forEach(System.out::println);
```

Cleaner and shorter.

---

# Types of Method References

There are **4 types**.

---

## 1. Static Method Reference

```java
ClassName::staticMethod
```

Example:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Demo {

    public static int square(int x){
        return x * x;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args){

        List<Integer> list = List.of(1,2,3,4);

        list.stream()
            .map(Demo::square)
            .forEach(System.out::println);

    }
}
```

---

## 2. Instance Method of Particular Object

```java
object::methodName
```

Example:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args){

        List<String> list = List.of("java","spring");

        list.forEach(System.out::println);

    }
}
```

---

## 3. Instance Method of Arbitrary Object

```java
ClassName::instanceMethod
```

Example:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args){

        List<String> list = List.of("java","spring","react");

        list.stream()
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .forEach(System.out::println);

    }
}
```

---

## 4. Constructor Reference

```java
ClassName::new
```

Example:

```java
import java.util.function.*;

public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args){

        Supplier<ArrayList<String>> supplier = ArrayList::new;

        ArrayList<String> list = supplier.get();

        list.add("Java");

        System.out.println(list);

    }
}
```

---

# Lambda vs Method Reference

Lambda:

```java
list.forEach(x -> System.out.println(x));
```

Method reference:

```java
list.forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

# Key Points

```
Uses :: operator
Shorter than lambda
Improves readability
Works with functional interfaces
```

---

# One-line Interview Answer

**A method reference is a shorthand notation using `::` to refer to an existing method or constructor instead of writing a lambda expression.**

## Dynamic Polymorphism in Java

**Dynamic Polymorphism = method call resolved at runtime based on the actual object type.**

It is achieved using **method overriding**.

Also called:

```text
Runtime Polymorphism
```

---

## Basic Example

Parent class:

```java
class Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }

}
```

Child class:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

}
```

Another child:

```java
class Cat extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Cat meows");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a1 = new Dog();
        Animal a2 = new Cat();

        a1.sound();
        a2.sound();

    }

}
```

Output

```text
Dog barks
Cat meows
```

---

## Why It Is Called Dynamic

Because the JVM decides **which method to execute at runtime**.

```text
Animal a = new Dog();

compile time → reference type = Animal
runtime      → object type = Dog
```

So JVM calls:

```text
Dog.sound()
```

---

## Runtime Method Dispatch

Example:

```java
class Shape {

    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing shape");
    }

}

class Circle extends Shape {

    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing circle");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Shape s = new Circle();
s.draw();
```

Output

```text
Drawing circle
```

JVM selects **Circle.draw()** at runtime.

---

## Key Requirements

Dynamic polymorphism requires:

```text
Inheritance
Method overriding
Parent reference → child object
```

Example pattern:

```java
Animal a = new Dog();
```

---

## Compile-Time vs Runtime

| Type | Example |
|---|---|
| Compile-time polymorphism | Method overloading |
| Runtime polymorphism | Method overriding |

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Dynamic polymorphism in Java is the ability of a parent reference to call different implementations of a method depending on the actual object type at runtime, typically achieved through method overriding.**

