## Abstract Class vs Interface (Java)

Both are used to achieve **abstraction**, but they differ in **design, capabilities, and usage**.

---

## Abstract Class

An **abstract class** can contain **both abstract and concrete methods**.

Example:

```java
abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }

}
```

Child class:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a = new Dog();

        a.sound();
        a.eat();

    }

}
```

---

## Interface

An **interface defines a contract** that classes must implement.

Example:

```java
interface Payment {

    void pay(double amount);

}
```

Implementation:

```java
class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("UPI Payment: " + amount);
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Payment p = new UpiPayment();
p.pay(500);
```

---

## Key Differences

| Feature | Abstract Class | Interface |
|---|---|---|
| Methods | Abstract + concrete | Abstract (default/static allowed in Java 8+) |
| Variables | Instance variables allowed | Only `public static final` |
| Constructors | Allowed | Not allowed |
| Inheritance | `extends` | `implements` |
| Multiple inheritance | Not supported | Supported |
| Access modifiers | Any | Methods public by default |

---

## Example Showing Multiple Interfaces

```java
interface A {
    void show();
}

interface B {
    void display();
}

class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        System.out.println("show");
    }

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("display");
    }

}
```

---

## When to Use

Use **Abstract Class** when:

```text
Classes share common behavior
Need constructors
Need instance variables
```

Use **Interface** when:

```text
Define capability/contract
Multiple inheritance required
Loose coupling needed
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**An abstract class can contain both abstract and concrete methods with instance variables, while an interface defines a contract with methods that implementing classes must provide, supporting multiple inheritance.**

## Suppressed Exception (Java)

A **suppressed exception** occurs when **multiple exceptions happen but one exception hides another**.

This commonly happens in **try-with-resources**.

Java stores the hidden exception as a **suppressed exception instead of losing it**.

---

## Example Without Try-With-Resources

```java id="a33mzy"
class Test {

    static void method() throws Exception {

        try {

            throw new Exception("Exception in try");

        } finally {

            throw new Exception("Exception in finally");

        }

    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        method();

    }
}
```

Output:

```text id="u7wqqc"
Exception in finally
```

The **try exception is lost**.

---

## Example With Try-With-Resources

```java id="0yujxf"
class Resource implements AutoCloseable {

    public void close() throws Exception {
        throw new Exception("Exception while closing resource");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java id="p3l61x"
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        try(Resource r = new Resource()) {

            throw new Exception("Main exception");

        }

    }

}
```

Output:

```text id="t1b3o5"
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.Exception: Main exception
Suppressed: java.lang.Exception: Exception while closing resource
```

Meaning:

```text id="ymbdpk"
Main exception → primary
Close exception → suppressed
```

---

## Accessing Suppressed Exceptions

```java id="yrbszc"
try {

    try(Resource r = new Resource()) {

        throw new Exception("Main exception");

    }

} catch(Exception e){

    System.out.println(e);

    for(Throwable t : e.getSuppressed()){
        System.out.println("Suppressed: " + t);
    }

}
```

---

## Why Suppressed Exceptions Exist

Without suppressed exceptions:

```text id="5b99g0"
Some exceptions would be lost
```

Java preserves them for **better debugging**.

---

## Key Methods

```java id="spdfve"
Throwable.addSuppressed()
Throwable.getSuppressed()
```

---

## Where Suppressed Exceptions Occur

Mostly in:

```text id="rmtz4r"
Try-with-resources
AutoCloseable resources
Stream/file/database operations
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**A suppressed exception is an exception that occurs during resource closing in try-with-resources and is preserved by the JVM instead of overriding the main exception.**

