## Diamond Problem (Java 8)

👉 **Definition (crisp)**  
Diamond problem occurs when a class implements **multiple interfaces having same default method**, causing ambiguity.

---

## Problem Example ❌

```java id="dp1"
interface A {
    default void show() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

interface B {
    default void show() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }
}

class Test implements A, B {
    // ❌ compile-time error
}
```

👉 Error:
```java id="dp2"
class Test inherits unrelated defaults for show() from A and B
```

---

## Solution 1: Override Method ✅

```java id="dp3"
class Test implements A, B {

    @Override
    public void show() {
        System.out.println("Resolved");
    }
}
```

---

## Solution 2: Call Specific Interface 🔥

```java id="dp4"
class Test implements A, B {

    @Override
    public void show() {
        A.super.show(); // or B.super.show()
    }
}
```

---

## Key Rule 🔥

```java id="dp5"
// Class must override if multiple default methods conflict
```

---

## Why This Happens?

👉 Java does NOT support multiple inheritance of classes  
👉 But default methods create **method-level multiple inheritance**

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Diamond problem occurs when **multiple interfaces provide the same default method, and the implementing class must override it to resolve ambiguity**.

