## How Java Resolves Default Method Conflicts?

👉 When multiple interfaces have same default method, Java uses **3 rules** 🔥

---

## Rule 1: Class Wins Over Interface ✅

```java id="r1"
interface A {
    default void show() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

class Test {
    public void show() {
        System.out.println("Class");
    }
}

class Demo extends Test implements A {}

new Demo().show(); // Class
```

---

## Rule 2: Most Specific Interface Wins ✅

```java id="r2"
interface A {
    default void show() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

interface B extends A {}

class Test implements B {}

new Test().show(); // A (via B)
```

---

## Rule 3: Explicit Override Required (Conflict Case) 🔥

```java id="r3"
interface A {
    default void show() { System.out.println("A"); }
}

interface B {
    default void show() { System.out.println("B"); }
}

class Test implements A, B {

    @Override
    public void show() {
        A.super.show(); // or B.super.show()
    }
}
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="r4"
// 1. Class method > interface default
// 2. Child interface > parent interface
// 3. Conflict → must override
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Java resolves default method conflicts using rules:  
**class methods override interfaces, more specific interface wins, and conflicts must be explicitly resolved by overriding**.

