## Why were Default Methods introduced? (Java 8)

---

## 1. Backward Compatibility (Main Reason 🔥)

👉 Interfaces couldn’t be changed earlier without breaking implementations

```java id="dmr1"
// OLD
interface A {
    void run();
}
```

👉 If we add new method:

```java id="dmr2"
interface A {
    void run();
    void log(); // ❌ breaks all classes
}
```

---

## Solution with Default Method ✅

```java id="dmr3"
interface A {
    void run();

    default void log() {
        System.out.println("Logging...");
    }
}
```

👉 No need to update existing classes

---

## 2. Support Functional Programming (Streams API)

```java id="dmr4"
interface Collection {
    default Stream stream() {
        // implementation
    }
}
```

👉 Allowed adding `stream()` to all collections

---

## 3. Code Reusability

```java id="dmr5"
interface Logger {
    default void log(String msg) {
        System.out.println(msg);
    }
}
```

👉 Shared implementation across classes

---

## 4. Interface Evolution

👉 APIs can grow without breaking old code

---

## 5. Reduce Utility Classes

```java id="dmr6"
// Instead of helper classes → logic inside interface
```

---

## Key Problems Solved 🔥

- Breaking changes in interfaces  
- API evolution issues  
- Code duplication  

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Default methods were introduced to **allow adding new methods to interfaces without breaking existing implementations, enabling backward compatibility and API evolution**.

