**Short answer (interview-ready):**  
Java Reflection API allows a program to inspect and manipulate classes, methods, fields, constructors, and annotations **at runtime**, even if they are private or unknown at compile time.

---

### 🔹 Core Idea (code-first)

```java
Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("java.lang.String");

// methods
Method[] methods = clazz.getDeclaredMethods();

// fields
Field[] fields = clazz.getDeclaredFields();

// constructors
Constructor<?>[] constructors = clazz.getDeclaredConstructors();
```

---

### 🔹 Invoke method dynamically

```java
class User {
    private String sayHello(String name) {
        return "Hello " + name;
    }
}

User user = new User();

Method method = User.class.getDeclaredMethod("sayHello", String.class);
method.setAccessible(true); // access private method

String result = (String) method.invoke(user, "Prem");
System.out.println(result);
```

---

### 🔹 Modify private field

```java
class User {
    private String name = "Old";
}

User user = new User();

Field field = User.class.getDeclaredField("name");
field.setAccessible(true);
field.set(user, "New");

System.out.println(field.get(user)); // New
```

---

### 🔹 Create object dynamically

```java
Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("java.util.ArrayList");
Object obj = clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();

System.out.println(obj.getClass()); // class java.util.ArrayList
```

---

## 🔥 Problems it solves (real-world)

### 1. **Frameworks (Spring, Hibernate, JUnit)**
```java
// Spring example (concept)
for (Field field : clazz.getDeclaredFields()) {
    if (field.isAnnotationPresent(Autowired.class)) {
        field.setAccessible(true);
        field.set(obj, dependency);
    }
}
```

👉 Used for:
- Dependency Injection
- Bean creation
- ORM mapping

---

### 2. **Unknown classes at runtime (Plugin systems)**

```java
Class<?> plugin = Class.forName("com.app.PluginImpl");
Object instance = plugin.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
```

👉 Load classes dynamically without recompiling

---

### 3. **Annotation Processing (Runtime)**

```java
if (method.isAnnotationPresent(Test.class)) {
    method.invoke(instance);
}
```

👉 Used in:
- JUnit
- Spring Boot
- Custom frameworks

---

### 4. **Serialization / Deserialization**

```java
for (Field field : obj.getClass().getDeclaredFields()) {
    field.setAccessible(true);
    map.put(field.getName(), field.get(obj));
}
```

👉 Used in:
- Jackson
- Gson

---

### 5. **Breaking Encapsulation (controlled use)**

```java
field.setAccessible(true); // bypass private
```

👉 Useful for:
- Testing
- Framework internals

---

## ⚠️ Downsides

```java
// slower than direct call
method.invoke(obj);

// no compile-time safety
Class.forName("WrongClass"); // runtime error
```

- Slower (no JVM optimizations)
- Unsafe (breaks encapsulation)
- Harder to debug

---

## 🧠 One-line summary (best for interviews)

👉 *“Reflection lets Java inspect and manipulate program structure at runtime, enabling frameworks, dynamic loading, and annotation-driven behavior.”*

